{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (via an automated cobbler-scaffold orchestrator) has assigned task #5449: implement `cmd/csplit` covering srd068 requirements R1.1-R1.4 (pattern-based splitting). This involves creating two files:\n   - `cmd/csplit/main.go` - The csplit utility implementation\n   - `cmd/csplit/csplit_test.go` - Differential tests against `gcsplit` (GNU reference binary)\n   \n   The task must satisfy acceptance criteria AC1-AC4 (build, vet, test, SRD requirements). Git operations are managed externally - no git commands should be run.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **csplit**: Splits a file into pieces at context-determined boundaries (regex patterns or line numbers)\n   - **Differential testing**: Uses `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` to compare Go binary output against GNU `gcsplit` reference binary\n   - **SIGPIPE handling**: Must call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at start of main\n   - **Pattern types (R1.1-R1.4)**:\n     - `/REGEXP/` - Split at next line matching REGEXP; matching line becomes first line of next piece (R1.2)\n     - `%REGEXP%` - Skip to matching line without creating output file for skipped content (R1.3)\n     - `INTEGER` - Split at given line number; that line becomes first line of next piece (R1.4)\n   - **Default output naming**: Files named xx00, xx01, xx02, etc. (R3.1, needed for basic functionality)\n   - **Error handling**: Exit 0 on success, exit 1 on pattern match failure or invalid options\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd068-csplit.yaml` (READ)\n     - This is the SRD defining all requirements for csplit\n     - R1 (Pattern-based splitting): R1.1-R1.4 are the scope of this task\n     - R2 (Repeat counts/offset): R2.1-R2.4 - NOT in scope but exist in SRD\n     - R3 (Output control/prefix): R3.1-R3.4 - R3.1 (default naming) needed for basic functionality\n     - R4 (Exit codes/testing): R4.1-R4.4 - R4.1 (exit 0 on success) needed for basic functionality\n     - Non-goals: --suppress-matched beyond basic, --keep-files on error\n     - Key acceptance criteria:\n       ```\n       AC1: printf 'a\\nb\\nc\\nd\\n' | csplit - /c/ produces xx00 (a,b) and xx01 (c,d)\n       AC2: seq 1 10 | csplit - 4 7 produces xx00 (1-3), xx01 (4-6), xx02 (7-10)\n       ```\n   \n   - `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` and `pkg/testutils/build.go` (PROVIDED in context)\n     - DiffTest struct with Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, ExpectedFiles fields\n     - `RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests)` runs differential comparison\n     - `BuildBinary(t, \".\")` compiles cmd package and returns binary path\n   \n   - `pkg/sys/sigpipe.go` (PROVIDED in context)\n     - `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` - must be called at start of main()\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet - implementation has not started.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Read the SRD file to understand requirements R1.1-R1.4 before implementation\n   - Identified that while only R1.1-R1.4 are in scope, basic output naming (R3.1 default xx00/xx01) and exit code handling (R4.1) are implicitly needed for the utility to function\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The massive task prompt containing the full project context, execution constitution, coding standards, task description for implementing cmd/csplit srd068 R1.1-R1.4, with constraints on not running git commands, writing all code before building, etc.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/csplit/main.go` implementing:\n     - R1.1: Accept one or more PATTERN arguments applied in order to split input\n     - R1.2: `/REGEXP/` pattern splits at matching line (match starts next piece)\n     - R1.3: `%REGEXP%` pattern skips to match without creating output file\n     - R1.4: `INTEGER` pattern splits at given line number\n     - SIGPIPE handler, default xx00/xx01 output naming, basic exit codes\n   - Create `cmd/csplit/csplit_test.go` with differential tests against `gcsplit`\n   - Run `go build -o bin/csplit ./cmd/csplit/` to verify compilation\n   - Run `go vet ./cmd/csplit/...` to check for issues\n   - Run `go test ./cmd/csplit/... -count=1` to verify tests pass\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the SRD file (`docs/specs/software-requirements/srd068-csplit.yaml`) and was about to begin implementing the two files. No code has been written yet. The implementation plan involves:\n   - Writing main.go with argument parsing (FILE and PATTERN args), line reading, pattern matching (regex and integer), file output with default xx00 naming\n   - Writing csplit_test.go with differential tests covering regex split, line number split, skip patterns, stdin input, and error cases\n   - The function size limit is 40 lines (E8), file limit 500 lines (E9)\n   - Must follow copyright header, SIGPIPE protocol, differential testing protocol\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write `cmd/csplit/main.go` and `cmd/csplit/csplit_test.go` implementing srd068 R1.1-R1.4 (pattern-based splitting). The task description states:\n   \n   - \"R1.1: Must accept one or more PATTERN arguments. Each pattern is applied in order to split the input at the matching boundary.\"\n   - \"R1.2: /REGEXP/ must split at the next line matching REGEXP. The matching line becomes the first line of the next piece.\"\n   - \"R1.3: %REGEXP% must skip to the next line matching REGEXP without creating an output file for the skipped content.\"\n   - \"R1.4: INTEGER must split at the given line number. The specified line becomes the first line of the next piece.\"\n   \n   Key implementation notes: csplit GNU behavior prints byte counts of each output file to stdout. Default prefix is \"xx\", default digit width is 2. Files are removed on error (non-goal says --keep-files not