ard.\n\nLooking at the test cases more carefully, the pattern is becoming clearer: regex patterns write the remaining content as a piece (printing its byte count) before erroring, while skip patterns don't create output files so they just error with no stdout. Integer patterns probably follow the regex behavior.\n\nI also notice the stderr normalizer is too narrow—it's looking for \"gcsplit:\" but the reference binary reports itself with its full path \"/opt/homebrew/bin/csplit:\", so I need to handle that.\n\nUpdating the code to handle regex no-match by emitting remaining content as an incomplete piece...\n\nActually, I need to think through the cleanup flow here. When `apply` writes a piece and then errors, `process` will remove all created files including that just-written piece, which is the correct behavior for error handling.\n\nFor the error message normalization, I'm using a regex to strip the binary name prefix more robustly since the reference implementation might report a full path like \"/opt/homebrew/bin/csplit:\" while my version just says \"csplit:\".\n\nWriting error normalizer... Writing regex patterns...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1005,"cache_read_input_tokens":73462,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1005},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45d3aba9-7d8f-4e0d-9de7-7f8799bb40c2","uuid":"e8cb2903-a2ae-4ea0-a29a-10aa798c46b6"}
